- in_text_citation: [(Vorobev et al., 2019-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/657fd1a6d80d911100025694e6575a2de8234d5f)
- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 54
[method]
The paper used a community-level high-throughput metagenomic and metatranscriptomic workflow to reconstruct eukaryotic marine plankton transcriptomes and characterize their functional potential. The approach involved sequencing metagenomic and metatranscriptomic data from eukaryotic plankton communities, reconstructing transcriptome units referred to as MGTs, and then applying taxonomic assignment, abundance computation, canopy clustering, completeness and contamination assessment, functional annotation, and comparison with reference transcriptomes. Functional analysis focused on pathway-level capabilities, including DMSP biosynthesis and degradation, and on identifying potential interspecies associations. This method relates to the proposed Ukiyo-e-Seq research plan because both aim to link eukaryotic plankton transcriptomes to taxonomy and function without relying on cultured isolates, but the paper operates at bulk community resolution rather than pairing single-organism optical images with single-well transcriptomes.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not contain numerical read counts, assembly statistics, binning metrics, or statistical significance values. Based on the section headings, the method produced an MGT collection that resolved taxonomic diversity, separated Bathycoccus ecotypes, characterized the biogeography of the genus Oithona, and generated functional insights including DMSP synthesis and degradation capabilities and candidate interspecies associations. These outcomes show that metagenomic and metatranscriptomic reconstruction can reveal eukaryotic plankton functional traits, but they do not provide organism-level optical identity or single-cell transcriptomic coverage.
[context]
The experimental context was eukaryotic marine plankton communities analyzed by high-throughput metagenomics and metatranscriptomics, with methods for sampling, abundance computing, canopy clustering, taxonomic assignment, completeness and contamination assessment, functional characterization, and comparison with reference transcriptomes. The provided excerpt does not include optical microscopy, robot-assisted single-cell capture, Smart-seq2, single-well RNA sequencing, or specific sequencing depth details. The paper therefore provides a community-level baseline for transcriptome reconstruction and functional pathway analysis, whereas the proposed method seeks paired optical and transcriptomic data from individual uncultured eukaryotic plankton.
[end related work 1]

