The paper describes an adapted MASC-seq workflow for single-cell transcriptomic analysis of microbial eukaryotic plankton. The method uses microarray slides to immobilize individual plankton cells, followed by cell fixation, permeabilization, in situ fluorescent cDNA synthesis, imaging of spots, cell removal, and library preparation. The workflow includes optimization of fixation, freeze-thaw permeabilization, attachment, staining, imaging, cell removal, automated library processing, and final library amplification, indexing, and sequencing. Data analysis assigns reads to microarray spot coordinates and compares single-cell profiles with bulk RNA sequencing of Heterocapsa sp. This relates to Ukiyo-e-Seq because both aim to connect optical/spatial information with transcriptomic data from individual eukaryotic plankton, but the paper uses microarray-based in situ cDNA synthesis and spot imaging rather than microscope-mounted capillary picking, environmental uncultured samples, Smart-seq2, and merge-split assembly.
[results]
The supplied excerpt does not provide quantitative outcomes, read depths, cell recovery rates, expression metrics, or statistical comparisons. It indicates that the MASC-seq method was adapted for eukaryotic single-celled plankton and applied to individual plankton species, but no numerical performance results are available in the provided text.
[context]
The experimental context includes cultured plankton cells, microarray slides, RNA extraction from cell cultures, optimization microarray experiments, cell fixation, freeze-thaw cycles, attachment/staining/imaging, permeabilization, cell removal, library preparation, automated library processing, final library amplification/indexing/sequencing, and bulk RNA sequencing of Heterocapsa sp. as a reference. The workflow is constrained by the need to attach cells to microarray spots, preserve RNA during fixation/permeabilization, image fluorescent cDNA signals, remove cells without losing material, and assign sequencing reads to the correct spot coordinates.
