[method]
The paper used a plate-based single-cell Smart-seq2 workflow in which individual cells were sorted or captured into 384-well plates, lysed in single wells, and cDNA was amplified to generate single-cell RNA-seq libraries. The workflow was applied to dermal fibroblasts and PBMCs that had been stimulated to elicit antiviral responses, and the resulting reads were processed through alignment, gene counting, and quality-control filtering. This is directly related to the proposed Ukiyo-e-Seq method, which also prepares single-well Smart-seq2 libraries in 384-well plates, but the proposed method applies the same single-well transcriptomic capture to environmentally collected plankton rather than sorted human cells and adds a merge-split assembly strategy for organisms without reference genomes.
[results]
The available paper information does not report numeric library-preparation or sequencing outcomes for the Smart-seq2 step, such as the number of cells passing QC, median read counts, gene counts, or duplicate rates. The broader reported outcome is that the single-cell Smart-seq2 data supported characterization of antiviral cell-state dynamics and dynamic eQTL mapping, including fine-mapping of the OAS1 locus associated with SARS-CoV-2 infection; no specific quantitative values for the Smart-seq2 method itself are available in the supplied text.
[context]
The paper's Smart-seq2 method was performed in a 384-well, single-cell format on stimulated human dermal fibroblasts and PBMCs. The exact donor numbers, stimulation time points, sequencing platform, read depth, and QC thresholds are not specified in the provided excerpt, so those reproducibility details cannot be stated. The proposed method differs in sample source and analysis: it targets captured plankton, uses 66 sample wells plus 4 negative controls, and plans approximately 250,000 paired-end reads per well on an Illumina NextSeq 500.
