[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Chen et al., 2011-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e7436408467bb48fcc1ea4fbb657f50b4177ddae)
- venue: Science China Life Sciences
- citation count: 17
[method]
The paper benchmarked three de novo transcriptome assembly strategies on two human reference RNA-seq datasets. Inputs were single-end 35 bp Illumina reads from Universal Human Reference RNA, representing 10 human cell lines with approximately 59.46 million reads, and Human Brain Reference RNA, representing 23 adult brain donors with approximately 53.24 million reads. Human hg19 genome sequences were also used as reference context. For each assembly strategy, contigs shorter than 100 bp were discarded. One strategy used VelvetOptimiser to search k-mer lengths from 17 to 33 and select an optimal k-mer and coverage cutoff; the other two strategies are not named in the provided text. The evaluation compared contig count, contig length range, N50, maximum contig length, total assembled length, and contig length distributions. This method is related to the proposed merge-split strategy because both rely on de novo transcriptome assembly to create a contig-based reference when species-specific genomes are unavailable, although the paper tests bulk human reference RNA rather than pooled single-well marine transcriptomes and does not implement the proposed well-specific alignment matrix.
[results]
For the brain transcriptome, the first strategy produced the largest number of contigs but the shortest maximum contig length, ranging from 100 to 3336 bp. The VelvetOptimiser strategy produced contigs ranging from 100 to 8474 bp. The third strategy produced the fewest contigs, 79,515, but the longest maximum contig, 12,895 bp. For the cell-line transcriptome, the first strategy produced contigs ranging from 100 to 3182 bp, the VelvetOptimiser strategy produced contigs ranging from 100 to 6087 bp, and the third strategy produced 79,367 contigs with a maximum length of 9,534 bp. N50 values were 244, 300, and 479 bp for the three brain assemblies, and 249, 303, and 513 bp for the three cell-line assemblies. Total assembled length was largest for the first strategy, at 25.2 Mb for brain and 26 Mb for cell lines, and smallest for the VelvetOptimiser strategy, at 18.4 Mb for brain and 19.6 Mb for cell lines. The third strategy generated fewer but longer and more continuous contigs, while the first and second strategies yielded shorter and more discontinuous contigs.
[context]
The datasets were human MAQC reference RNA samples sequenced as single-end 35 bp Illumina reads, with approximately 53 to 59 million reads per dataset. The human hg19 reference genome was used as background reference material. Assembly evaluation applied a 100 bp minimum contig length. VelvetOptimiser searched k-mer lengths from 17 to 33 and selected k = 25 with a coverage cutoff of 2.83 for both brain and cell-line assemblies. No CPU, memory, runtime, or full assembler names beyond VelvetOptimiser are provided. The experimental context is human reference RNA rather than environmental plankton, so the findings are indirect for the proposed oceanic single-well merge-split workflow, but they illustrate how de novo transcriptome assembly choices affect contig continuity, total assembled length, and potential redundancy.
[end related work 2]

[start related work 3]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Chen et al., 2011-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1c4baeb20f8868b1f134683cec53b213bbef88a6)
- venue: Science China Life Sciences
- citation count: 70
[method]
The paper describes genome-independent transcriptome reconstruction for RNA-Seq, in which short reads are assembled directly into transcripts without requiring a species-specific reference genome. The approach uses de Bruijn graph-based assembly, where reads are decomposed into overlapping k-mers, the graph is parsed, and contigs or scaffolds are produced. The paper names Velvet, Trans-ABySS, Trinity, and Oases as examples of genome-independent transcriptome assemblers, and contrasts this with genome-guided methods such as Cufflinks and Scripture, which first map reads to a reference genome and then reconstruct transcripts from spliced alignment information. This relates directly to the proposed merge-split strategy in the research plan, which uses rnaSpades to pool reads from all single-well plankton samples, de novo assemble a common contig reference, and then align reads from each well separately to that reference to build a sample-by-contig expression matrix. In both cases, the key idea is to create a usable transcriptomic reference from the data themselves when no high-quality species-specific genome is available.
[results]
The provided paper sections do not report quantitative experimental outcomes, benchmark values, statistical significance, runtime, accuracy, sensitivity, precision, or expression quantification metrics for the transcriptome reconstruction methods. The reported outcomes are qualitative: genome-independent methods can be used for species without available reference genomes, but they mainly reconstruct transcripts expressed at moderate or high abundance, while low-abundance transcripts are difficult to recover unless sequencing depth is high. Repetitive and homologous sequences can cause read-mapping ambiguity in genome-guided methods and assembly collapses in genome-independent methods. The paper also notes that even well-studied model genomes may be incomplete or contain misassembled regions, and it cites a previous finding that a significant number of human genes were missing from the human reference genome but were detected using human brain tissues and 10 mixed cell lines. No numerical performance comparison between Velvet, Trans-ABySS, Trinity, Oases, Cufflinks, and Scripture is provided.
[context]
The context is a review of RNA-Seq applications rather than a primary experimental study, so no dataset size, sequencing platform, hardware specifications, k-mer size, assembly parameters, or evaluation metrics are specified. The relevant constraints are biological and computational: reference genome availability, reference genome quality and completeness, transcript length, sequencing depth, count distribution among samples, repetitive genomic sequences, homologous isoform sequences, and expression level. The paper states that genome-guided methods are best when a high-quality and relatively complete reference genome is available, while genome-independent methods are more reasonable when no reference genome exists. This supports the research plan’s merge-split design for uncultured oceanic plankton, where most species lack reference genomes and single-well transcriptomes must first be assembled into a shared de novo reference before per-well read alignment and contig-level quantification.
[end related work 3]

[start related work 4]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Lu et al., 2013-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c1187edee50091dff9da539dfdc34bbf1746a642)
- venue: Science China Life Sciences
- citation count: 76
[method]
The paper's relevant method is a comparative benchmark of transcriptome reconstruction strategies, focusing on de novo assembly and reference-guided assembly. Inputs were three Illumina RNA-Seq datasets: human brain, mouse embryonic stem cells, and S. pombe, with known reference genomes and annotations. De novo assemblers Trinity, Oases, and trans-ABySS were run on filtered reads; genome-guided assemblers Cufflinks and Scripture were run on spliced alignments from TopHat, with Cufflinks also run in reference-annotation-based mode. De novo outputs were mapped to reference genomes with BLAT, filtered for at least 95 percent identity over at least 80 percent of transfrag length, and unaligned transfrags were checked against protein databases. Assembly outputs were evaluated for accuracy, completeness, contiguity, chimerism, variant resolution, runtime, memory, transfrag number and length, read usage, and agreement with reference annotation. Read usage was measured by aligning input reads back to assembled transfrags, which is analogous to the proposed matrix of well-specific reads per contig. This method relates to the proposed merge-split strategy because the plan uses de novo assembly of pooled single-well reads to build a common contig reference and then aligns each well separately to build an expression matrix; the paper tests whether de novo assembly can produce usable transcript contigs without a species-specific reference and how reference-guided alignment changes completeness, contiguity, artifact rate, and read usage.
[results]
Quantitative and comparative outcomes were dataset-dependent. For accuracy, trans-ABySS was highest on mouse and S. pombe but lowest on human brain; Oases was much lower on mouse; Trinity was poorest on S. pombe. Long polyA tails were present in many de novo transfrags: human brain had 17694 trans-ABySS and 4152 Oases transfrags with polyA tails of at least 35 bp; mouse had 1035 Oases and 209 trans-ABySS; S. pombe had 72 Oases and 3 trans-ABySS. Unaligned transfrags still had protein hits: Oases had 2479 hits on mouse and 1310 on S. pombe; Trinity had 2118 and 1900; trans-ABySS had 8020 and 96. Genome-guided assemblers had higher completeness and contiguity on shallow human brain data, but de novo assemblers improved faster with depth and reached or exceeded genome-guided values on ultra-deep S. pombe data; among de novo assemblers Trinity had the largest completeness and contiguity, followed by Oases and trans-ABySS, except S. pombe where trans-ABySS completeness was slightly higher than Oases. Cufflinks outperformed Scripture, and Cufflinks with reference annotation had the best completeness and contiguity but was not 100 percent. Genome-guided assemblers produced more fused transcripts, especially on S. pombe, while de novo chimerism was below 1 and Oases was below 0.25. De novo assemblers generally reported more isoforms per gene on human brain and mouse, consistent with their higher transcript counts per locus. De novo assemblers used more time and memory; Oases used the most memory but ran faster, Trinity used long time and large memory and disk, trans-ABySS used less memory and disk, and Cufflinks and Scripture used much less than de novo tools. De novo assemblers produced more transfrags, often shorter and more redundant; trans-ABySS produced many fragments below 100 bp, Oases produced the longest de novo fragments, and Oases and Scripture were highly redundant. Oases used much fewer reads than Trinity and trans-ABySS, and read usage increased with sequencing depth. Compared with annotation, de novo assemblers detected more transcripts and loci, especially Oases and trans-ABySS; Trinity detected more loci and fewer transcripts per locus; Scripture detected more transcripts and loci than Cufflinks; Cufflinks with reference annotation found more transcripts per locus and had high base sensitivity and specificity. Genome-guided assemblers generally had higher specificity, while sensitivity varied: Cufflinks was highest on human brain and mouse, Trinity was higher on S. pombe, and Scripture was higher on mouse. Genome-guided assemblers found more full-length transfrags except on S. pombe; Cufflinks with reference annotation produced much more full-length and fewer partial transfrags; Scripture reported many full-length transfrags but only 2435, 3809, and 1574 known transcripts were covered entirely by a single Scripture transfrag on human brain, mouse, and S. pombe, respectively. Among de novo assemblers, Trinity identified the most full-length and fewest partial transfrags, trans-ABySS recovered the most partial transfrags, Oases identified more novel transfrags, and trans-ABySS found the fewest. Across FPKM quantiles, Cufflinks with reference annotation reconstructed almost all transcripts completely on all datasets, while other assemblers reached that level only on ultra-deep S. pombe at higher expression; median transcript coverage generally increased with expression, but most assemblers dropped at the highest expression level, with Scripture showing the largest drop. No statistical significance tests were reported.
[context]
Experimental conditions included bulk RNA-Seq, not single-well or single-cell data, and all three species had reference genomes and annotations, so the paper does not directly test the no-reference merge-split workflow or RNA-SPAdes. Coverage was approximately 22x for human brain, 50x for mouse embryonic stem cells, and 499x for S. pombe. Human brain data came from 23 adult donors and multiple brain regions; mouse data were embryonic stem cells; S. pombe data were late stationary phase. Reference annotations were filtered to autosomes, MT, X, and Y for human and mouse. Reads were quality filtered with fastx_toolkit for the single-end brain data and Sickle with quality threshold 2 for the paired-end mouse and S. pombe data, and checked with FastQC. Assembly software versions included Cufflinks 2.0.0, Scripture VPaperR3, ABySS 1.3.3, trans-ABySS 1.3.2, Trinity r2012-04-27, Velvet 1.2.03, and Oases 0.2.06, with Velvet 1.2.07 used for mouse and S. pombe to avoid merging paired-end files. Oases and trans-ABySS used eight k-mers selected by manual and read length; trans-ABySS used only the FEM step. Genome-guided assembly used TopHat spliced alignments; BLAT was tested for S. pombe but not used because it showed no clear advantage. De novo validation used BLAT with default parameters except S. pombe max intron length 900, then pslCDna-Filter requiring 95 percent identity over 80 percent of transfrag length, and BLASTX against nr with e-value 1e-35 for unaligned transfrags. Overlap metrics used intersectBed with split and width options, and chimerism additionally required 50 percent overlap and strand sensitivity. Expression analysis used Cufflinks FPKM values greater than 0.0005 divided into 20 quantiles. Computations ran on a 512 GB NUMA server with a 2.67 GHz Intel Xeon CPU, 48 cores, and CentOS Linux 6.0. These conditions matter for the proposed plan because single-well transcriptomes may have lower effective coverage and more contamination than bulk RNA-Seq, so the paper's high-coverage de novo success does not guarantee full-length reconstruction from individual wells, while its observation that reference-guided alignment improves completeness and contiguity supports aligning each well to a pooled de novo reference.
