[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Xie et al., 2013-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d67a93295497578e114125a2e5e1041a1ec91fb4)
- venue: Bioinform.
- citation count: 878
[method]
The paper evaluated SOAPdenovo-Trans, a de Bruijn graph-based de novo transcriptome assembler adapted for RNA-Seq by incorporating error removal and alternative-splicing handling concepts from Trinity and Oases. The method assembled contigs from paired-end RNA-Seq reads and then performed scaffold construction by mapping reads back onto contigs to infer linkages. It used both single-end and paired-end read information, weighted linkages by read counts, and used paired-end insert sizes to estimate distances between contigs. This is relevant to the proposed merge-split strategy because the proposed method first pools reads from many single-well plankton samples and builds a common de novo transcriptome reference with rnaSpades before aligning each well separately; the paper provides a benchmarked example of de novo transcriptome assembly and contig-to-contig scaffolding from pooled RNA-Seq, and it highlights how read mapping, contig redundancy, and alternative-splice-like fragments can affect the quality of the assembled reference used for downstream per-sample alignment and expression counting.
[results]
On rice and mouse paired-end Illumina datasets, SOAPdenovo-Trans was competitive in runtime and memory, with peak memory of 10.7 GB and 0.2 h for rice small, 29.3 GB and 0.8 h for rice large, 10.5 GB and 0.3 h for mouse small, and 16.7 GB and 1.0 h for mouse large. It produced fewer transcripts than Trinity and Oases after filtering to >300 bp: 25,800 versus 35,690 and 36,145 for rice small; 38,789 versus 54,546 and 92,381 for rice large; 16,286 versus 29,449 and 34,767 for mouse small; and 25,037 versus 43,892 and 49,624 for mouse large. Correctness was higher: 91.8% versus 83.6% and 83.1% for rice small; 89.5% versus 80.9% and 82.1% for rice large; 98.0% versus 93.9% and 94.1% for mouse small; and 97.1% versus 92.8% and 93.0% for mouse large. Chimeric rates were lower: 2.0% versus 5.7% and 6.0% for rice small; 2.6% versus 7.6% and 5.5% for rice large; 1.0% versus 4.2% and 2.8% for mouse small; and 1.8% versus 5.4% and 3.7% for mouse large. When putative alternative splice forms were excluded, recovered gene counts were similar across assemblers, for example 14,651, 14,120, and 13,413 for rice small and 10,409, 10,488, and 10,016 for mouse large. Length-ratio cumulants were closer to the ideal step function near 100% for SOAPdenovo-Trans, and the paper reported that 1.83 Gbp of mouse data outperformed 5.49 Gbp assembled by Trinity. Submaximal transcript analysis showed many redundant or fragmented assemblies with little new exon content, indicating that apparent alternative splice forms can be assembly artifacts.
[context]
The benchmark used curated rice and mouse reference genomes and paired-end Illumina RNA-Seq data in small and large datasets. Rice assemblies were affected by subspecies mismatch between indica transcriptomes and japonica annotations, with close to 10% of assemblies failing to align to the japonica genome until combined subspecies genomes were used; the paper retained japonica-only alignments for simplicity. Evaluations restricted assemblies to >300 bp, required BLAT alignments at 95% identity, defined correct assemblies as having at least 95% of length in one consistent alignment, and defined chimeric assemblies as having at least 95% of length in two or more alignments with different orientations, chromosomes, or distal regions. Series A included putative alternative splice forms, while Series B excluded them by retaining the longest sequence per locus. About 30.3% of rice annotated loci were transposable elements, and non-TE percentages were reported to show TEs were not the main driver. Computations used 10 threads on two quad-core Intel 2.8 GHz CPUs with 70 GB memory running CentOS 5. This context is relevant to the proposed Ukiyo-e-Seq plan because the proposed method will assemble pooled single-well plankton reads without species-specific references and then align each well separately; the paper shows that de novo transcriptome assembly can be evaluated against a reference, but it does not test single-well low-coverage data, the merge-split pooling strategy, or taxonomic and functional annotation of uncultured plankton.
