[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Xie et al., 2013-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d67a93295497578e114125a2e5e1041a1ec91fb4)
- venue: Bioinform.
- citation count: 878
[method]
The paper evaluates SOAPdenovo-Trans, a de Bruijn graph-based transcriptome assembler derived from a genome assembler, for bulk paired-end RNA-seq. It performs contig assembly and transcript assembly, with scaffold construction by mapping reads back onto contigs. Both single-end and paired-end read information are used to build linkages, read counts are used to weight linkages, and paired-end insert sizes are used to estimate distances between contigs. This is related to the proposed Ukiyo-e-Seq plan because both approaches rely on de novo assembly of transcriptomic reads without a species-specific reference and on aligning reads to assembled contigs. However, the paper uses bulk rice and mouse RNA-seq data and reference genomes for benchmarking, not single-well plankton reads, rnaSpades, or a contig-by-well expression matrix. The paper’s read-to-contig mapping is used for scaffold construction rather than for sample-wise expression quantification.
[results]
SOAPdenovo-Trans was computationally competitive. Peak memory and runtime were 10.7 GB and 0.2 h for rice small, 29.3 GB and 0.8 h for rice large, 10.5 GB and 0.3 h for mouse small, and 16.7 GB and 1.0 h for mouse large. Trinity used 10.1 GB and 17.7 h, 37.6 GB and 35.6 h, 10.5 GB and 16.6 h, and 26.3 GB and 47.5 h, respectively. Oases used 9.0 GB and 0.6 h, 53.2 GB and 3.0 h, 8.8 GB and 0.7 h, and 35.1 GB and 2.7 h, respectively. SOAPdenovo-Trans produced the fewest transcripts longer than 300 bp: 25,800 for rice small versus 35,690 for Trinity and 36,145 for Oases; 38,789 for rice large versus 54,546 and 92,381; 16,286 for mouse small versus 29,449 and 34,767; and 25,037 for mouse large versus 43,892 and 49,624. Correct assembly rates were 91.8% for rice small, 89.5% for rice large, 98.0% for mouse small, and 97.1% for mouse large. Chimeric rates were 2.0% for rice small, 2.6% for rice large, 1.0% for mouse small, and 1.8% for mouse large. Series-B non-TE counts were 14,090 for rice small, 16,874 for rice large, 9,442 for mouse small, and 10,409 for mouse large. The assembled-to-annotation length cumulant was closer to the ideal step function than for Trinity or Oases, and the authors argued that 1.83 Gbp of mouse data outperformed Trinity with 5.49 Gbp. Submaximal transcripts often had near 1:1 length ratios and little new exon overlap, indicating artefactual redundancy rather than true alternative splicing.
[context]
The experiments used paired-end Illumina RNA-seq from rice and mouse, in small and large datasets, benchmarked against extensively curated reference genomes and annotations. Rice used indica transcriptomes aligned to japonica annotations, causing about 10% of assemblies to fail alignment; using combined indica and japonica genomes removed this issue. Assemblies longer than 300 bp were aligned with BLAT at 95% identity. Assemblies were considered correct when at least 95% of their length could be accounted for in one consistent alignment, and chimeric when at least 95% of their length could be accounted for in two or more alignments with different orientations, on different chromosomes, or in distal regions of the same chromosome. Series A included putative alternative splice forms, while Series B excluded them by retaining the longest isoform per locus. Runs used 10 threads on two quad-core Intel 2.8 GHz CPUs with 70 GB of memory and CentOS 5. The context is bulk transcriptome assembly, not single-cell or metatranscriptomic plankton data, so it does not directly validate the proposed merge-split single-well expression matrix, taxonomic classification, ORF annotation, or AlphaFold structural analysis.
