[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Wood et al., 2019-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1553161499f48202696cb1fac9f6ca0f006dc6b0)
- venue: Genome Biology
- citation count: 6706
[method]
The relevant method in the paper is Kraken2 taxonomic classification. Kraken2 builds a compact hash table from a reference database by extracting spaced seeds and minimizers from each reference genome and storing the associated taxon identifier. For a query sequence fragment, it extracts the same spaced seeds/minimizers, performs exact lookups in the hash table, and assigns the lowest common ancestor of all matching taxa. The method also supports translated search for protein-coding sequences by considering translated frames, which is relevant when classifying cDNA or transcriptomic reads. The paper evaluated this classifier on a standard genomic reference library, strain-exclusion datasets, FDA-ARGOS metagenomic reads, thread-scaling benchmarks, database-size comparisons against Kraken1, and Bracken abundance estimation. This relates directly to the proposed research plan because Ukiyo-e-Seq uses Kraken2 against large metagenomic and metatranscriptomic databases to assign taxonomic identities to single-well transcriptomes when reference genomes are unavailable.
[results]
Kraken2 was reported to be approximately 50-fold faster than Kraken and approximately 100-fold faster than BLAST while maintaining comparable accuracy. In a large benchmark, it classified about 100 million 100 bp reads in roughly 47 minutes. On strain-exclusion data, Kraken2 assigned most reads to the correct genus and a high proportion to the correct species, with performance comparable to Kraken and BLAST. On FDA-ARGOS experimental data, it achieved high concordance with expected taxa, reported at approximately 96% at species level and higher at coarser taxonomic ranks. Thread scaling was near-linear, with a speedup close to the number of threads up to 8 threads. Kraken2 databases were smaller than equivalent Kraken1 databases, reported around 1.4 GB versus 2.5 GB for the same reference library.
[context]
The paper’s evaluations used large metagenomic reference libraries and benchmark datasets rather than single-well eukaryotic plankton transcriptomes. Key conditions included a standard genomic reference library, strain-exclusion datasets generated by withholding strains, FDA-ARGOS metagenomic reads, default spaced-seed and minimizer parameters, and a multi-core Intel Xeon system with up to 8 threads and sufficient RAM for multi-gigabyte hash tables. The proposed method would apply the same Kraken2 classifier to individual well cDNA reads, but the paper does not directly validate performance on uncultured eukaryotic plankton single-well transcriptomes or on databases dominated by metatranscriptomic sequences.
[end related work 1]

