[method]
The paper evaluated taxonomic read classification using Kraken2 by constructing multiple Kraken2 reference databases and classifying reads from a simulated known-truth dataset. Database construction used kraken2-build to download standard libraries for bacteria, archaea, viral, and human sequences, and in some databases added UniVec_Core, RUG draft genomes from /rug_drafts/*.fna, and Hungate reference genomes from /hungate_genomes/*.fasta. The tested databases included a minimal standard database, a standard database with UniVec_Core, a RUG-augmented database, a Hungate-augmented database, and a combined Hungate/RUG database. Kraken2 databases were built with 16 threads. This method is directly relevant to the proposed Ukiyo-e-Seq workflow because the proposal plans to use Kraken2 against large metagenomic and metatranscriptomic databases to assign taxonomic identities to single-well transcriptomes when reference genomes are unavailable; the paper tests how the choice and composition of the Kraken2 reference database affects classification performance.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not report numeric classification rates, accuracy values, or statistical significance. It reports that classification rate and classification accuracy were strongly affected by the reference database used, that the composition of the reference database impacted both taxonomic read classification accuracy and inferred taxonomic read abundance, and that adding genomes from cultured isolates derived from the environment of study greatly increased classification rate and accuracy. It also reports that metagenome-assembled genomes could further improve classification but were limited by poorly defined taxonomy. These outcomes indicate that for single-well transcriptome classification, database selection is a major source of variation and that environment-specific references are likely important.
[context]
The experimental context was a controlled simulation with known ground truth rather than real single-well transcriptomes. The main variable was reference database content: standard Kraken2 libraries, UniVec_Core, RUG draft genomes, and Hungate genomes. Kraken2 database builds used 16 threads. The evaluation focused on metagenomic read classification and abundance estimation, not optical pairing or single-cell transcriptomics. For the proposed research plan, this context means that Kraken2-based taxonomic assignment of 384-well Smart-seq2 transcriptomes should be benchmarked against multiple databases, especially those containing marine or environment-specific eukaryotic and prokaryotic references, and that MAG-derived references may improve assignment only if their taxonomy is sufficiently resolved.
[end related work 2]

