- venue: PLoS ONE
- citation count: 19
[method]
The paper used a field validation of an autonomous in-situ biosampler by comparing manual Ocean Sampling Day filtration with the autonomous system and then profiling the recovered microbial communities by 16S and 18S metabarcoding. Surface seawater was collected in washed buckets, stored in two prewashed 20 L carboys, and 3 L was filtered through 0.22 µm Sterivex-GP units in triplicate. The manual procedure used a diaphragm vacuum pump generating approximately 1 bar differential pressure, while the autonomous system used a self-priming peristaltic pump, microcontroller, flow sensor, manifold, pressure gauge, and filter cartridge, tested at approximately 1.0 bar and 1.3 bar. Filters were stored at -80°C, DNA was extracted with a PowerWater kit, quantified by Qubit fluorometry, amplified for the 16S V4-V5 region and the 18S V4 region, sequenced on an Illumina MiSeq platform, and analyzed with mothur preprocessing, VSEARCH 97% OTU clustering, QIIME rarefaction, Bray-Curtis similarity clustering, and ANOVA/Tukey/Simprof comparisons. This method relates to the proposed Wiley's Baths plankton-net sampling because both are pilot environmental tests on real marine plankton communities, but the paper tests filtration-based eDNA recovery and community-level metabarcoding rather than net-based size selection, single-cell imaging, or single-cell RNA sequencing.
[results]
For 2 L filtration, the autonomous system reduced filtration time to 24 ± 1 min compared with 35.8 ± 0.3 min for the manual procedure. Increasing pump pressure from 0.8 to 1.3 and 1.8 bar increased average filtration flow and reduced filtration time, although flow decreased significantly as filtered volume or filtration time increased. After filtering 3 L at 1 bar, eDNA yield was not significantly different between the manual and autonomous procedures, and no significant difference was found between autonomous filtration at 1 bar and 1.3 bar, although the 1.3 bar condition showed greater standard deviation. Metabarcoding produced 540,677 and 266,117 raw read pairs for 16S and 18S libraries, curated to 462,956 and 227,045 unique sequences, and clustered into 385,029 and 149,725 OTUs. Libraries were rarefied to 23,574 reads for 16S and 7,654 reads for 18S. No significant differences were detected between filtration procedures in phylum-level composition, lower-taxonomy community structure, rare-biosphere diversity indices, or rarefaction curves; only minor non-significant shifts were observed.
[context]
The samples were coastal surface seawater collected in November 2016 approximately 25 km offshore from the northwest Portuguese coast. Filtration was performed in triplicate, with simultaneous manual and autonomous runs and carboys shaken before each filtration to maintain homogeneity. The 0.22 µm pore size captured particles larger than 0.22 µm, which is much finer than the proposed 25 µm to 1 mm size window and plankton-net collection, so the paper is relevant as a real-community sampling and workflow validation but not a direct match to single-cell eukaryotic plankton capture. Key constraints included 3 L per filter, 1.0 and 1.3 bar autonomous filtration pressures, approximately 1 bar manual vacuum pressure, -80°C filter storage, Illumina MiSeq paired-end sequencing, 97% OTU clustering, removal of singletons and metazoans, and statistical evaluation using ANOVA, Tukey tests, Bray-Curtis similarity, and Simprof tests.
[end related work 1]

