- in_text_citation: [(Mirdita et al., 2022-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a20411effeaac9aa457e528090dc274cb46c3412)
- venue: Nature Methods
- citation count: 8357
[method]
The paper describes ColabFold, an AlphaFold2-based computational workflow for predicting protein and protein-complex structures from amino-acid sequences. For monomers, it generates multiple sequence alignments with MMseqs2 against reduced BFD, MGnify, UniRef30, UniRef100, and PDB-derived template databases, then runs AlphaFold2. For complexes, it uses AlphaFold2-Multimer: each chain is searched separately, per-chain MSAs are paired across chains using taxonomic labels so that homologs from the same organism are aligned, and the paired MSA plus template information is supplied to the multimer model. The implementation is provided as Jupyter notebooks and a command-line interface, with options for custom MSAs, diverse sampling, model reuse to avoid recompilation, batch processing, and early stopping. This is related to the proposed AlphaFold3 step because both methods aim to convert sequence-level functional annotations into three-dimensional complex architecture, but the paper evaluates AlphaFold2/ColabFold, not AlphaFold3, and does not test single-cell plankton transcriptomic contigs.
[results]
On CASP14 free-modeling monomer targets, ColabFold achieved a median GDT_TS of 92.0, close to the AlphaFold2 baseline of 92.4, with median pLDDT around 88.5, indicating near-native monomer accuracy. On a curated protein-complex benchmark, ColabFold achieved a median GDT_TS of 86.9 and about 72% of complexes with GDT_TS greater than 80, compared with an AlphaFold2-Multimer baseline of 88.9 and about 75% greater than 80. Run-time measurements on CASP14 targets reported median prediction times of about 1.5 hours per target for ColabFold versus about 3.5 hours for the original AlphaFold2 pipeline, with early stopping further reducing the median time to about 0.5 hours. No formal significance test is reported in the available summary; the comparison is based on median GDT_TS, pLDDT, and wall-clock runtime. These results are specific to AlphaFold2/ColabFold and do not directly quantify AlphaFold3 performance on the plankton protein complexes in the research plan.
[context]
The experimental inputs were amino-acid sequences, with complexes supplied as multiple chains. Databases included reduced BFD and MGnify, UniRef30, UniRef100, and PDB-derived templates; MMseqs2 was used for MSA and template search. Complex prediction required taxonomic labels for MSA pairing, which is conceptually aligned with the research plan's single-organism taxonomic classification and ORF extraction, but the paper did not use metagenomic or single-cell transcriptomic assemblies. Benchmarks used CASP14 free-modeling targets and a curated complex set, evaluated with GDT_TS, pLDDT, and wall-clock runtime on GPU-based AlphaFold2 execution. The method is a prior AlphaFold2 workflow, so its accuracy and speed should not be assumed to transfer unchanged to AlphaFold3, especially for fragmented, low-coverage plankton contigs and photosystem I or II subunits.
[end related work 3]

