- in_text_citation: [(Ryck et al., 2023-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/60d572e3569ea40fc0f7724a6721eed0526583a0)
- venue: Frontiers in Microbiology
- citation count: 15
[method]
The paper discusses AlphaFold and AlphaFold-Multimer as computational structural prediction tools for extending functional annotation of predicted proteins. AlphaFold is described as a neural-network method that predicts three-dimensional structures of individual protein chains using amino acid sequence, multiple sequence alignments, and homology to known proteins. AlphaFold-Multimer is described as an extension that predicts structures for multiple chains, thereby enabling modeling of protein-complex architecture and potentially mapping effector-host protein interactions. The paper also notes that predicted or defined structures can be compared with Protein Data Bank entries or species-specific AlphaFold database subsets using DALI. This is directly related to the proposed research method, which plans to use AlphaFold 3 to predict three-dimensional structures of protein complexes from individual plankton organisms after transcriptomic annotation, especially for photosystem I and photosystem II complex subunits. The paper’s use case is bacterial type III effector structural annotation, whereas the proposed plan applies the same general strategy to single-organism eukaryotic plankton transcriptomes.
[results]
No experimental outcome from AlphaFold or AlphaFold-Multimer is reported in the paper. The paper does not provide AlphaFold-predicted structures, confidence scores, complex accuracy metrics, or comparisons to experimentally determined structures. The only structural-prediction-related experimental outcomes reported are for Phyre2, not AlphaFold: Phyre2 analysis of RipAK supported domain deletion experiments showing that full-length RipAK was required for hypersensitive response suppression in tobacco, and Phyre2 analysis of RipAY revealed structural similarity to gamma-glutamyl cyclotransferase and conserved catalytic residues, which guided mutational analyses that identified gamma-glutamyl cyclotransferase activity. These Phyre2 results are relevant as analogous structure-guided functional annotation, but they are not direct results of the proposed AlphaFold 3 complex-prediction method.
[context]
The paper’s context is functional characterization of Ralstonia solanacearum type III effectors in plant-pathogen systems, not marine plankton. It states that direct de novo sequence submission to AlphaFold was not available at the time, so the Collabfold source-code route could be used, and it positions AlphaFold-Multimer as a future tool for multi-chain complex prediction. Structural comparison would require a predicted or defined PDB file and could be performed against the Protein Data Bank or a species subset such as Arabidopsis. The proposed research plan differs in biological system and input data: it uses single-well RNA sequencing from individually picked uncultured eukaryotic plankton, de novo contig assembly, ORF prediction, functional annotation, and KEGG-based identification of photosystem I and II components, then applies AlphaFold 3 to infer complex architecture from those organism-specific sequences. The paper therefore provides conceptual and methodological precedent for using AlphaFold-family tools to extend functional annotation into structural and complex-level interpretation, but it does not demonstrate the plankton-specific workflow or provide quantitative validation of AlphaFold 3 complex predictions.
[end related work 4]

